## Imported documents in Cardmaniacs

Beyond web articles, Cardmaniacs imports **local files** as first-class cards:

- **PDF**
- **EPUB**
- **Markdown** (`.md`)

Imported documents appear in Read Later collections filtered by type—**PDFs**, **Markdown**, **Books** (EPUB)—and open in the reader with format-appropriate controls.

## Import paths

- **File → Import** or **Import…** (`⌘⇧I`) on macOS
- **Drag and drop** onto the app window or dock icon (macOS)
- **Share sheet** from Files or other apps (iOS/iPadOS)
- In-app welcome guides and document import flows for first-time setup

The import pipeline extracts title, cover or preview metadata, and reading position where supported.

## Local files vs synced metadata

{% important %}
File bytes and sync behavior depend on document type and platform settings. Metadata—title, tags, read progress, highlights—syncs through iCloud; large PDFs may remain primarily on the device that imported them until opened elsewhere.
{% /important %}

Check **Settings → Advanced → Data & Storage** for storage-related options and export paths.

## Reading PDFs

PDF cards support:

- Page navigation and zoom
- Highlights (see [Highlights and Tags](/docs/highlights-and-tags))
- Reader themes where applicable
- **Separate reader window** on macOS for side-by-side reference

Use **Go to PDFs** in the command palette to filter the queue to PDF documents only.

## Reading EPUB

EPUB imports map to the **Books** collection. Long-form navigation, typography controls, and progress sync apply similarly to web articles. Paged mode (`⌘⇧P`) may suit long chapters on macOS.

## Markdown files

Markdown imports appear under **Go to Markdown** collections. Rendering respects reader typography settings; code-heavy files benefit from wide content width (palette: Content Width: Wide).

## Documents vs URL bookmarks

| URL bookmark | Imported file |
|--------------|----------------|
| Points to live web | Local copy on disk |
| Reader extracts HTML | Native PDF/EPUB/MD rendering |
| May break if site changes | Stable offline reading |

For archival PDFs (papers, reports), import the file. For frequently updated docs, a bookmark may be better.

## Welcome guides

The app ships **in-app welcome guides** (markdown bundled with the app) explaining import basics. Website documentation expands those seeds; content is not auto-synced from the app bundle in v1.

## Troubleshooting

- **Import failed** — confirm file type and size; try Import… again from a local path
- **Missing on second device** — open Files sync settings; wait for iCloud; re-import if needed
- **Garbled PDF text** — scanned PDFs may not extract for search; highlights still apply to selections

